@aojea @pohly  These events were added by https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/20607 which provides more debug information for https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/issues/20547. This issue was resolved but the event is still left in the controller. 

I'm unsure whether it is right to remove those events, switch to print some logs instead, or keep it as is. Leave it to the reviewer/author of the above issue or sig-cloudprovider to decide. I'll update the code later if needed.

/cc @bprashanth @mikedanese @thockin

@aojea but cloud vendors use `app.NewCloudControllerManagerCommand` to create a cloud controller manager. The `recorder` field may not initialized when event handler receives events. And the `recorder` field is private.  It can not make sure the `service controller` initialized during the `cloud controller manager` setup.

https://grep.app/search?q=app.NewCloudControllerManagerCommand

![image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/6f7cc7cb-3b8b-4db0-9036-412a37898164)

Let me reply here even though it doesn't belong into this PR:
> If the controller removed the event recorder field, it would become harder for callers to get the recorder.

I was thinking of a separate parameter, not storing it in the context - that wouldn't be very obvious. A better alternative might be to separate the recorder into a second struct:
```
struct Controller { 
    // no recorder here!
}

struct runningController {
   *Controller // embedded
   recorder ...
}

func (c *Controller) Run(ctx) {
    // construct recorder and runningController
}

func (c *runningController) someMethod(ctx) {
   // use c.recorder or any field in Controller, just as before.
}
```
